# What NOT to send to the Docker daemon as build context.
#
# Two separate reasons, and the second is the important one:
#   1. Speed — the whole context is tarred and uploaded to the daemon before
#      the first instruction runs. .venv/ alone is ~200 MB of Linux-useless
#      macOS binaries.
#   2. SAFETY — anything in the context can be baked into a layer by a careless
#      COPY, and layers are forever: deleting a file in a later RUN does not
#      remove it from the image, it is still there in the layer beneath and
#      `docker history` will show it. Secrets must never enter the context at
#      all.

# --- Secrets. Never in the context, never in a layer. -----------------------
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
.env
.env.local

# --- Runtime state. The database and retained card images belong in the
# --- mounted volume, not baked into the image. Copying them in would ship one
# --- user's extracted personal data to everyone who pulls the image.
data/

# --- Host-specific and useless in Linux ------------------------------------
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.DS_Store

# --- Version control. .git/ is often larger than the app itself, and it
# --- contains every version of every file ever committed.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# --- Docs and fixtures: not needed to run the server -------------------------
README.md
samples/
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
